      "text": "To find the smallest positive integer that is both an integer power of 11 and is not a palindrome, we can use the reasoning outlined in the provided solution.\n\nFirst, we note that any power of 11 will never be a palindrome. This is because the decimal representation of any power of 11, when written out, contains both odd and even digits (since \\(11^1 = 11\\), \\(11^2 = 121\\), and so on). Each digit being the result of a sum of two single-digit and single-digit numbers (if the same number is a palindrome) is insufficient to form a palindrome because it would break the symmetry needed for a palindrome. This holds true irrespective of the position of the digit, as a symmetry in the place of the digit is a necessary condition for a number to be a palindrome.\n\nTherefore, the solution to this problem can be defined by any positive integer power of 11, as per the reasoning above. Since there are infinitely many positive integer powers of 11, it is implied that there is no smallest positive integer power of 11 that is not a palindrome, as that would contradict the non-existence of smaller powers of 11 that are not palindromes.\n\nHence, the smallest positive integer that is both an integer power of 11 and not a palindrome does not exist, but any positive integer power of 11 would suffice in the given context.",
