    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Since the power of a number represents the place value, the place value of the integer part of the obtained number must be 1. Therefore, the smallest positive integer that is a power of 11 and is positive (positive) is 11^1. Thus, the smallest positive integer that is both an integer power of 11 and is not a palindrome.\n\nTo confirm, let's look at the numerical values:\n\\[\n11^1 = 11\n\\]\nThere are 2 digits in 11. The places of the digits are in order. In particular, the units digit of the integer is 1, which is 1, so this integer is 11 and not a palindrome. Hence, this is the answer: \\boxed{21}.\n\nTo verify the accuracy of this solution, I will generate Python code. The program will determine for every integer power of 11, if the computation results are not palindromes. Then, it will determine the smallest such integer.\n```python\n# Create a list to hold results of not palindromes\r\nno_palindrome_numbers = []\r\n\r\n# Cycle through n = 1 to just under 10^3\r\nfor n in range(1, 100):\r\n    exp = 11 ** n\r\n    # Convert exponentiation result to string.\r\n    str_exp = str(exp)\r\n    if len(str_exp) <= 1:\r\n        # If either of these conditions is met, the result is already a palindrome,\r\n        # because it's a one-digit number or a zero-digit number.\r\n        continue\r\n    # Check whether the result is a palindrome by comparing it to its reverse\r\n    if str_exp == str_exp[::-1]:\r\n        continue\r\n\r\n    # If we haven't met the above conditions, add the computation result to our list\r\n    no_palindrome_numbers.append(exp)\r\nprint(no_palindrome_numbers)  # Print the list of results in order base 10\n```",
